<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<chapter xml:id="ch-comrades-marathon">
  <title>Over 90 years running 90 km</title>

  <introduction>
    <blockquote>
      <p>"Success supposes endeavour."</p>
      <p>--- Jane Austen</p>
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      <em>Background.</em> The Comrades Marathon between Durban and Pietermaritzburg has been run over ninety times
      since 1921.
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Questions.</em> How many people take part? Who takes part and how has that changed? How long do the best and
      typical runners take? Are men much faster than women? How do "up" and "down" races compare?
    </p>
    <p><em>Sources.</em> Data from Stratton with race-profile information from East.</p>
    <p><em>Structure.</em> 445,129 participants' runs with 12 variables.</p>
  </introduction>

  <section xml:id="sec-comm-entries">
    <title>How many run in the Comrades (Ultra)Marathon?</title>

    <p>
      The race began in 1921 with 16 finishers. Direction typically alternates by year: "down" (Durban to
      Pietermaritzburg) and "up" (the reverse), with a few historical exceptions.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-comdir">
      <image source="images/comDir-1.png"/>
      <caption>
        <p>Numbers of finishers and race direction by year (dark orange up, black down).</p>
      </caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Participation stayed low until the 1960s, rose sharply in the 1970s and 1980s, and spiked in 2000 due to heavy
      publicity and a one-year extension of the finishing limit from 11 to 12 hours.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-comm-age-sex">
    <title>Numbers by age and sex</title>

    <p>
      Women and black runners were officially allowed from 1975, though notable earlier exceptions occurred. Runners
      are grouped into age categories (senior, veteran, masters, grandmasters), enabling long-run composition trends.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-comcats">
      <image source="images/comCats-1.png"/>
      <caption>
        <p>
          Numbers of finishers by age group and sex from 1975 to 2019 (brown line indicates missing birth-year
          category).
        </p>
      </caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Male seniors dominated for many years; in recent years male veterans have approached similar numbers. Smaller
      groups generally increased, with visible peaks in 2000 and 2010.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-wfnos">
      <image source="images/wfNos-1.png"/>
      <caption><p>Numbers of female (purple) and foreign (red) finishers since 1975.</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Both female and foreign participation increased over time, with stronger variability in female counts.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-comm-finish-times">
    <title>How long do runners take to finish?</title>

    <p>
      Cross-year comparisons are affected by weather, route distance, race direction, and changing field sizes, along
      with historical time-limit changes.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-combox">
      <image source="images/comBox-1.png"/>
      <caption><p>Distributions of finishing times in hours by year.</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      The wartime gap (no races from 1941 to 1945) and the 11/12-hour limits are clear. Median finishing times
      generally rose as participation increased; 2000 stands out with a higher median and spread.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-comstat">
      <image source="images/comStat-1.png"/>
      <caption><p>Median times, best times, and numbers of finishers from 1946 to 2019.</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Best times have remained relatively stable in recent decades, while median times increased.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-comm-men-women">
    <title>Comparing times for men and women</title>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-comm-best-mf">
      <title>Best times for men and women</title>

      <figure xml:id="fig-commf">
        <image source="images/comMF-1.png"/>
        <caption><p>Best times for women (purple) and men (green) since 1980.</p></caption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        Men's best times are faster on average, and male finishers substantially outnumber female finishers.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-comfperc">
        <image source="images/comFperc-1.png"/>
        <caption><p>Percentage of women participants since 1980.</p></caption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        The female participation share rose steadily to around 2010, then stabilized near one fifth.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-comm-median-mf">
      <title>Median times for men and women</title>

      <figure xml:id="fig-commfmed">
        <image source="images/comMFmed-1.png"/>
        <caption>
          <p>
            Median times for men and women for up (dark orange) and down (black) races since 1980, with numbers
            finishing.
          </p>
        </caption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        Median times have generally increased (except the 2000 anomaly), and up races tend to be slightly slower than
        down races.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-comm-medals">
    <title>How many runners get medals?</title>

    <figure xml:id="fig-comtimes">
      <image source="images/comTimes-1.png"/>
      <caption>
        <p>
          Finishing times in the 2019 race for women (top) and men (bottom), with dotted medal-threshold lines.
        </p>
      </caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      The distributions show boundary effects at key target times, consistent with runners pacing to medal and
      cut-off thresholds.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-comm-up-down">
    <title>How different are the up and down races?</title>

    <figure xml:id="fig-comud">
      <image source="images/comUD-1.png"/>
      <caption><p>Comparisons of best times for up (dark orange) and down (black) races since 1921.</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Best times in up races are typically slightly higher than nearby down-race best times.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-comudp">
      <image source="images/comUDp-1.png"/>
      <caption>
        <p>
          Average speed of fastest and median runners (km/hr) for up (dark orange) and down (black) races since
          1970.
        </p>
      </caption>
    </figure>

    <figure xml:id="fig-comdist">
      <image source="images/comDist-1.png"/>
      <caption>
        <p>
          Distances for up and down races since 1970 and a planned 2020 down-race elevation profile.
        </p>
      </caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Down races are not continuously downhill and up races are not continuously uphill, but the net elevation
      difference between cities is substantial.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-comm-individuals">
    <title>How do individual runners perform over the years?</title>

    <p>
      Individual trajectories reveal both consistency and variability, including notable cases such as Elena and
      Olesya Nurgalieva.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-comnurg">
      <image source="images/comNurg-1.png"/>
      <caption><p>Performances of the Nurgalieva identical twins.</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Their performances were very close across many years, with several races separated by only small margins.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-commany">
      <image source="images/comMany-1.png"/>
      <caption><p>Runners with more than 40 finishes, ordered by first year raced.</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Many long-career runners show an improve-then-decline pattern with age, but individual variation is large.
    </p>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        <em>Answers.</em> Participation has grown dramatically; female and international participation increased;
        older-runner representation rose; best times improved then stabilized; median times rose with larger fields;
        men's best times remain faster by roughly three quarters of an hour; and up-race best times are generally
        slightly slower than down-race best times.
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Further questions.</em> How are age and performance related when full birth-year data are used? How do
        patterns compare with other marathons that are shorter and newer?
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-comm-takeaways">
      <title>Graphical takeaways</title>
      <ul>
        <li><p>Year-wise boxplots can convey rich distributional information compactly.</p></li>
        <li><p>Axes need not start at zero when comparisons remain honest and clear.</p></li>
        <li><p>Aligned time-series summaries plus counts are effective for historical context.</p></li>
        <li><p>Related plots can use different scales to reveal different aspects of structure.</p></li>
      </ul>
    </subsection>
  </section>
</chapter>
